# Review is mandatory

Any change that affects what the user sees or how the app behaves is not done
when the code compiles and tests pass. It is done after you verify it yourself
in a real browser with the `chrome-devtools` MCP. Never ask the user to be
your first reviewer.

This applies to UI, UX, store/cache logic, API wiring, and any other change
where the user would notice stale data, wrong state, or a crash. Unit tests
alone are not enough.

## Steps

1. Make sure the dev server is up: `pnpm dev:all`, app at `http://localhost:5173`.
2. Navigate to the screen that owns the changed behavior, then reach the exact
   state you changed. Select the task, switch tabs, drag a row, submit a form.
3. `take_snapshot` for structure and `take_screenshot` for pixels when UI changed.
   Read the screenshot. Do not assume it looks right.
4. Exercise the interaction end to end with `click`, `fill`, `press_key`,
   `hover`, drag. Confirm the observable result, not just that nothing threw.
5. `list_console_messages` must be clean. Any new warning or error is a defect
   of this change until proven otherwise.

## Functional changes to check every time

- Cache and refetch: after a mutation, does the UI update without a full page
  reload? Open the inspector, perform the action, confirm the new data appears.
- React hooks: no hooks after early returns or inside conditionals. If a query
  needs to be conditional, use `skip`, not an `if` guard around the hook call.
- Cross-tab state: switching tabs or selecting another task must not crash or
  leave stale panels visible.
- Optimistic updates: on failure, does the UI roll back cleanly?

## UI side effects to check every time

- Neighboring states of the same component: selected and unselected, empty and
  populated, completed and active, dragging and idle.
- Every bucket and route that renders the component, not only the one you were
  looking at.
- Alignment and baseline of siblings. A wrapper added for one element commonly
  shifts the row it lives in.
- Keyboard path and focus order, plus `aria-label` on any icon-only control.
- Motion: run the transition, then confirm no leftover inline `transform` or
  `transition` sticks to the node afterwards.

## Reporting

State what you opened, what you clicked, what you saw, and what you ruled out.
If devtools cannot reach a state, say so plainly and name the untested state.
Never claim a change is verified from unit tests alone.
